{{Short description|Second century Roman senator and consul}}
'''Lucius Bruttius Quintius Crispinus''' was a [[Roman Empire|Roman]] [[Roman Senate|senator]] who lived in the second century AD. He was [[Roman consul|ordinary consul]] in 187, and [[Lucius Roscius Aelianus Paculus]] was his colleague.<ref>Paul Leunissen, ''Konsuln und Konsulare in der Zeit von Commodus bis Severus Alexander'' (Amsterdam: Verlag Gieben, 1989), p.&nbsp;131</ref>

Crispinus was a member of the [[Bruttia gens]], who originated in Volceii, [[Lucania]], Italy.<ref>Leunissen, ''Konsuln und Konsulare'', p.&nbsp;356</ref> His father was [[Gaius Bruttius Praesens (consul 153)|Gaius Bruttius Praesens]], consul under [[Antoninus Pius]]; Olli Salomies suggests that his mother's name, which is otherwise unknown, was "Quintia" based on his ''nomen'' "Quintius", but Salomies has no further ideas about her identity.<ref>Salomies, ''Adoptive and polyonymous nomenclature in the Roman Empire'' (Helsinki: Societas Scientiarum Fennica, 1992), p.&nbsp;107</ref> Crispinus' paternal grandparents were the consul and senator [[Gaius Bruttius Praesens Lucius Fulvius Rusticus|Gaius Bruttius Praesens]], and [[Laberia Hostilia Crispina]], the daughter of [[Manius Laberius Maximus]].<ref>Leunissen, ''Konsuln und Konsulare'', p.&nbsp;371</ref> His sister was [[Bruttia Crispina]], who married the Emperor [[Commodus]].

He is generally thought to be the father of [[Gaius Bruttius Praesens (consul in 217)|Gaius Bruttius Praesens]], consul in 217.<ref>Leunissen, ''Konsuln und Konsulare'', p.&nbsp;373</ref>

